g memory load reduces the efficiency of attentional control, leading to greater susceptibility to distraction.\n    *   **Epistemic Status:** This is a core theoretical proposition within the WLM framework, particularly in its interaction with attention theories (e.g., Lavie's load theory). It is structurally motivated by the idea that attentional resources are finite and shared with working memory. This claim has been extensively empirically validated, even if not formally derived within the WLM framework itself.\n    *   **(1) Specific claims validated:** High working memory load (e.g., maintaining a complex digit string) reduces the ability to ignore irrelevant distractors in a concurrent perceptual task, leading to increased distractor interference.\n    *   **(2) By whom:** Nilli Lavie and colleagues are prominent researchers in this area, but countless others have replicated and extended these findings.\n    *   **(3) With what experimental methodology:** Behavioral experiments using dual-task paradigms. Participants perform a primary working memory task (e.g., n-back, digit span) while simultaneously performing a secondary task (e.g., visual search, flanker task) that includes distractors. Distractor interference is measured (e.g., reaction time difference between congruent and incongruent trials).\n    *   **(4) In which publication:** Lavie, N., Hirst, A., de Fockert, J. W., & Viding, E. (2004). Load theory of selective attention and cognitive control. *Journal of Experimental Psychology: General*, *133*(3), 339-354. (This is a seminal review, but specific empirical papers by Lavie and colleagues, such as de Fockert, J. W., Rees, G., Frith, C. D., & Lavie, N. (2001). The role of working memory in visual selective attention. *Science*, *291*(5509), 1803-1806, provide direct empirical validation.)\n\n2.  **Claim:** Different types of information (e.g., verbal, visuospatial) are processed and maintained by relatively distinct, but interacting, working memory subsystems, each with its own limited capacity.\n    *   **Epistemic Status:** This is a foundational theoretical proposition of multi-component models of working memory (e.g., Baddeley & Hitch's model), which informs the WLM framework. It is structurally motivated by observed dissociations in cognitive performance under different types of load. This claim has been extensively empirically validated.\n    *   **(1) Specific claims validated:** Interference effects are stronger when two tasks draw on the same working memory subsystem (e.g., two verbal tasks) than when they draw on different subsystems (e.g., one verbal, one visuospatial).\n    *   **(2) By whom:** Alan Baddeley, Graham Hitch, and their numerous collaborators and followers.\n    *   **(3) With what experimental methodology:** Dual-task paradigms where participants perform two tasks concurrently. The tasks are designed to specifically tax either verbal (e.g., digit span, phonological loop tasks) or visuospatial (e.g., spatial span, visual patterns test, visuospatial sketchpad tasks) working memory. Interference is measured by performance decrements on one or both tasks.\n    *   **(4) In which publication:** Baddeley, A. D., & Hitch, G. J. (1974). Working memory. In G. A. Bower (Ed.), *The psychology of learning and motivation: Advances in research and theory* (Vol. 8, pp. 47-89). Academic Press. (This is the original theoretical proposal, but subsequent empirical work, e.g., Baddeley, A. (1986). *Working memory*. Oxford University Press, and many later papers, provide extensive validation.)\n\nHas WLM been empirically validated? Yes.\n\nJustification: As demonstrated in section (C), specific theoretical propositions central to the WLM framework, such as the impact of working memory load on attentional control and the existence of distinct, capacity-limited working memory subsystems, have been rigorously tested and supported by extensive empirical research using established experimental methodologies in 