ations, and severity of cardiac disease, to assess comparability and potential confounding.\n- Data on specific adverse events leading to mortality from [REPORT B].\n- Long-term (5-year) all-cause mortality data from [REPORT A] to compare directly with [REPORT B]'s timeframe.\n- Data on the specific definition of 'cardiac events' in [REPORT A] and how it relates to 'all-cause mortality' in [REPORT B].\n- Data on dose, duration of treatment, and adherence in [REPORT B].\n\nPart (e): Clinician's question:\nThe answer is 'cannot determine'. [CANNOT DETERMINE FROM GIVEN DATA]\n\nEvidence required to determine:\n1. An RCT specifically designed to assess all-cause mortality in patients over 65 with elevated cardiac risk, comparing Drug X to a placebo or standard of care, with a follow-up period of at least 5 years. This would directly address the specific patient population and the critical outcome of mortality, minimizing confounding.\n2. Failing a new RCT, a re-analysis or sub-group analysis of [REPORT A] data, if available, focusing on patients over 65 and reporting all-cause mortality over 5 years.\n3. A meta-analysis of all available high-quality studies (especially RCTs) on Drug X, specifically examining age-stratified outcomes for both cardiac events and all-cause mortality over comparable follow-up periods.\n4. Mechanistic studies to understand how Drug X might reduce cardiac events while simultaneously increasing overall mortality, particularly in older individuals. This would help in risk-benefit stratification."
